-- @tmpfile's registry must follow the pool call, not the thread that happened
-- to call it. A manifold is free to spawn threads -- that is how a native
-- parallel map is written -- and a temp file created on one of them belongs to
-- the call, so the call may close it and the end-of-call sweep must reclaim it
-- if the call does not.
--
-- The registry is exercised through libmorloc's C ABI rather than through the
-- @tmpfile syntax, because the thread case only arises inside native code: a
-- morloc expression always evaluates on the thread that is running the
-- manifold.
--
-- What fails when the registry is per-thread: a file made on a spawned thread
-- cannot be closed from the manifold's thread, because the path is not in that
-- thread's list. The same split also strands the file from the end-of-call
-- sweep, which runs on the manifold's thread -- not asserted here, because a
-- nexus removes its whole tmpdir on exit and a one-call test cannot tell a
-- swept file from a discarded directory.

module main (closeHere, closeThere, closeUnregistered)

import root-cpp

source Cpp from "thr.hpp"
  ("closeHere", "closeThere", "closeUnregistered")

closeHere :: Int -> Str
closeThere :: Int -> Str
closeUnregistered :: Int -> Str
